{"version": "2.0", "head_chars": 5000, "group": "ls-LS16-S12-0000", "entries": [{"k": "questions|ls|16|12|STARRED   |1", "t": "Central Universities", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 1 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.07.2017 \n \nCentral Universities \n \n*1. SHRIMATI RAMA DEVI:  \n  \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government is facilitating establishment of a number of Central Universities \nunder the Central Universities Act, 2009 to address the regional imbalances in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) the names of these Universities established under Central Universities Act, 2009 along \nwith their location; and  \n(d) the names of Universities from among the above Universities which are under operation?\n  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n (a) to (d)  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 1 FOR 17.07.2017 ASKED BY SHRIMATI \nRAMA DEVI, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING CENTRAL \nUNIVERSITIES \n \n \n(a) & (b) At present there is no proposal to set up new Central Universities in the country. \nHowever, the Andhra Pradesh Re-organisatio n Act 2014 envisages establishment of one \nCentral Tribal University each in the State of Andhra Pradesh and Telangana and one Central \nUniversity in the successor state of Andhra Pradesh. Further, the Prime Minister announced a \npackage for Bihar on 18.8.2015 which includes establishment of a Central University near \nBhagalpur at the historic site of Vikramshila University. \n \nWith a view to increase the access and improve the quality of higher education and to \naddress the issue of regional imbalance in the country, the 11 th five Year Plan (2007-2012), as \napproved by the National Development Council (NDC), envisaged setting up of one Central \nUniversity each in States which did not have any Central Universities (CUs) till then. \nAccordingly, 16 CUs were established under the Central Universities Act, 2009 including 3 \nState Universities converted into Central Universities. \n \nDuring 12\nth Plan (2012-17) as approved by the NDC, the thrust was on consolidation \nof the higher education system. Expansion is to be mainly done by scaling up capacity of the \nexisting institutions in place of setting up new institutions. Besides, the Central Government \nhas launched Rashtriya Uchchatar Shiksha Abhi yan (RUSA) on cost sharing basis to assist \nStates to open new institutions. \n \nThe Central Universities are envisaged to func tion more as pace setter institutions to \nprovide academic leadership to other institutions in the region rather than as institutions to \nredress the regional imbalance. ‘Education’ being a concurrent subject, the State \nGovernments are also taking various initiatives to address the issue of regional imbalance in \nhigher education in the respective States. Besides, Private Universities/Institutions are also \nplaying a significant role in this regard.  \n \n(c) & (d) A list of Central Universities established under the Central Universities Act, 2009 \nalong with their location is given at Annexure I. All these Central Universities are operational.   \n**** \n   \n\n \nAnnexure-I \nState-Wise list of   new  and converted Central Universities established under Central \nUniversities Act, 2009  \nNew Central Universities \nBihar  \n1. Central University of South Bihar, Gaya. \n2. Mahatma Gandhi Central University, Motihari. \nGujarat \n3. Central University of Gujarat, Gandhinagar \nHaryana  \n4. Central University of Haryana, Mahendergarh. \nHimachal Pradesh \n5. Central University of Himachal Pradesh, Dharamshala. \nJammu & Kashmir \n6. Central University of Kashmir, Srinagar. \n7. Central University of Jammu, Jammu. \nJharkhand \n8. Central University of Jharkhand, Ranchi  \nKarnataka \n9. Central University of Karnataka, Gulbarga. \nKerala \n10. Central University of Kerala, Kasaragod. \nOrissa \n11. Central University of Orissa, Koraput \nPunjab \n12. Central University of Punjab, Bhatinda. \nRajasthan \n13. Central University of Rajasthan, Ajmer.  \nTamil Nadu \n14. Central University of Tamil Nadu, Thiruvarur. \nConverted Central Universities \nChhattisgarh \n15. Guru Ghasidas Vishwavidyalaya, Bilaspur.  \nMadhya Pradesh \n16. Dr. Harisingh Gour Vishwavidyalaya, Sagar.  \nUttarakhand \n17. Hemawati Nandan Bahuguna Garhwal University, Srinagar.  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|STARRED   |10", "t": "Maternity Benefit (Amendment) Act", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 10 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.07.2017 \n \nMATERNITY BENEFIT (AMENDMENT) ACT \n*10. SHRIMATI POONAM MAHAJAN:  \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate:  \n(a)whether the Government has issued guidelines for implementation \nof the Maternity Benefit (Amendment) Act, 2017;  \n(b)if so, the details thereof; \n(c)whether the Government is anticipating decline in rate of labour \nforce participation of female population in the country as the \nemployers might adopt discriminatory hiring practices in order to \nprevent maternity benefits under the said Act of 2017; and \n(d)if so, the action plan adopted/ proposed to be adopted by the \nGovernment to prevent discriminatory hiring practices?  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BANDARU DATTATREYA) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*                                                          ****** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.10 FOR 17.07.2017 BY SHRIMATI \nPOONAM MAHAJAN REGARDING MATERNITY BENEFIT (AMENDMENT) \nACT. \n \n(a) & (b):  Section 28 of the Maternity Benefit Act,1961 empowers the \nappropriate Government to frame Rules for effective implementation of \nthe Act. The Maternity Benefit (Mines and Circus) Rules,1963 are \nalready in place to guide implementation of the said Act. Under the \nMaternity Benefit (Amendment) Act, 2017, and with regard to newly \ninserted provisions rel ating to crèche facility, the Ministry of Labour \nand Employment has issued an intention Notification on 18 th May, 2017 \ncalling for suggestions/ objections from stakeholders on the said \nRules.  \n \n(c) & (d):  No, Madam. The Government is of opinion that with \nenforcement of the Maternity Benefit (Amendment) Act,  2017, more \nand more women workers would be inclined to seek employment \nwhich will lead to increase in women labour participation rate. There \nare stringent provisions under the Maternity Benefit Act, 1961 as well \nas in the Equal Remuneration Act, 1974 for prevention of \ndiscriminatory practices against women work force.   \n \n******* \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|STARRED   |100", "t": "Viral Fever", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 100\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 21ST JULY, 2017\nVIRAL FEVER\n†*100. PROF. RAVINDRA VISHWANATH GAIKWAD:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether a large number of people die of viral fever every year in the rural and\nurban areas of the country;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) the number of persons who died of viral fever in the cou ntry during the last three\nyears, State-wise; and\n(d) the steps taken by the Government in this regard so far?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (d) : A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 100* FOR 21ST JULY, 2017\n(a) to (c ):     It is true that many people are affected by Viral fever both in the\nRural and Urban areas of the Country.  Under the Integrated Disease Surveillanc e\nProgramme (IDSP) of the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, the National\nCentre for Disease Control (NCDC) collects data from the States and Union\nTerritories on the number of cases reported, deaths occurred etc.  During the year\n2014, the total numbe r of cases reported was 5582 and the number of death s was\n44.  During 2015, the numbers were 3585 and 15 and during 2016 the numbers\nwere 3037 and 19 respectively.  The State-wise data is as indicated at Annexure.\n(d): Health is a State subject. However, the Ministry of Health and Family\nWelfare, through the Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme , extends\nassistance to all the States and UTs to effectively detect and respond to disease\noutbreaks including viral fever by way of ` manpower, strengthening of\nlaboratories, Information & Communication Technology (ICT) , equipment and\nfunds etc. The National Centre for Disease Control also helps the States and UTs\nthrough surveillance, investigation, laboratory and other facilities. It also helps\nthe States in capacity building through training & developmental activities.\nHealth System Strengthening support is also being given to the State\nGovernments and UTs under National Health Mission (NHM) to enhance the\ncapacity of the Health Institutions up to the district level in the States/UTs.\n\nAnnexure\nState-wise Deaths Due to Viral Fever in India 2014 – 2016\nSl No State / U.T. 2014 2015 2016 (Prov.)\n1 Andhra Pradesh 1 0 0\n2 Arunachal Pradesh 0 0 0\n3 Assam 0 0 0\n4 Gujarat 3 0 0\n5 Haryana 0 0 0\n6 Himachal Pradesh 0 0 0\n7 J & K 0 0 0\n8 Jharkhand 0 0 0\n9 Karnataka 0 0 2\n10 Kerala 0 0 0\n11 Madhya Pradesh 0 0 0\n12 Maharashtra 37 12 9\n13 Manipur 0 0 0\n14 Meghalaya 0 0 0\n15 Odisha 0 0 1\n16 Rajasthan 0 0 0\n17 Tamil Nadu 0 0 1\n18 Telangana 2 0 2\n19 Uttarakhand 0 0 2\n20 Uttar Pradesh 0 3 2\n21 West Bengal 1 0 0\nTotal 44 15 19\nNote: 1.Cases and Deaths were reported by Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme (IDSP) at\nthe initiation of the outbreaks.\n2. No outbreaks of viral fever were reported under IDSP by the remaining States & UTs.\n*******"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|STARRED   |101", "t": "Trade Deficit", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT OF INDUSTRIAL POLICY & PROMOTION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 101. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, THE  17TH JULY, 2017. \n \nREVIVAL OF CLOSED GOVERNMENT FACTORIES \n \n101.  COL. SONARAM CHOUDHARY: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n                                          वाणिज् य वव  द्यग  ं ्री \n(a)  whether the Government factories  are running in commercial loss in several  \nStates and several others have been shut down due to losses; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n \n(c)  the details of the Government  factories shut down due to losses during  the \nlast three years, State/UT-wise; and  \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nवाणिज् य वव  द्यग  ााज् यं ्री स्‍ वतं ् ्रभााा(स्रींतंरी िरंमलाा ीरीतंाांि(  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nOF THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRIMATI NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n(a) to (d):  The details of Government factories running in losses are not \nmaintained by Department of Public Enterprises (DPE).  As per the \nPublic Enterprises Survey 2015 -16, that was laid on the Table of both \nthe Houses of Parliament on 21.3.2017, there are 78 Central Public \nSector Enterprises (CPSEs) which have incurred loss in 2015 -16. The \nMinistry-wise, State-wise (State, where registered office of CPSEs are \nlocated) details of loss making CPSEs are given in Annexure-I.  \n \nThe CPSEs suffer with sickness due to a number of factors including \npoor order book, shortage of working capital, surplus manpower, \nobsolete plant and machinery and difficulty to adjust to changing \nmarket conditions. \n \nAs per the information available with Department of Public Enterprises, \nthe details of CPSEs /units approve d for closure by the Government \nare given in Annexure-II. \n******** \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF THE LOK \nSABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 101 FOR ANSWER ON 17.07.2017. \n \nMinistry-wise loss making CPSEs with Profit After Tax (PAT) during 2015-16 \n   \nRs. In \nLakhs \nS. \nNo. Ministry/Department/CPSE \nNAME OF THE STATE \nWHERE REGISTERED \nOFFICE LOCATED PAT \n  DEPT. OF ATOMIC ENERGY     \n1 INDIAN RARE EARTHS LTD. MAHARASHTRA -10451 \n  MINISTRY OF AYUSH     \n2 \nINDIAN MEDICINES & PHARMACEUTICAL \nCORPN. LTD. UTTARANCHAL -332 \n  DEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS     \n3 \nBENGAL CHEMICALS & PHARMACEUTICALS \nLTD. WEST BENGAL -913 \n4 HINDUSTAN ANTIBIOTICS LTD. MAHARASHTRA -7718 \n5 IDPL (TAMILNADU) LTD. TAMILNADU -73 \n6 INDIAN DRUGS & PHARMACEUTICALS LTD. HARYANA -16608 \n7 \nRAJASTHAN DRUGS & PHARMACEUTICALS \nLTD. RAJASTHAN -599 \n  \nDEPARTMENT OF CHEMICALS & \nPETROCHEMICALS     \n8 HINDUSTAN ORGANIC CHEMICALS LTD. MAHARASHTRA -17391 \n9 HINDUSTAN FLUOROCARBONS LIMITED TELANGANA -1111 \n10 \nBRAHAMPUTRA CRACKERS & POLYMER \nLTD. ASSAM -27023 \n  DEPARTMENT OF FERTILIZERS     \n11 \nFERTILIZERS & CHEMICALS (TRAVANCORE) \nLTD. KERALA -45219 \n12 MADRAS FERTILIZERS LTD. TAMILNADU -18954 \n13 PROJECTS & DEVELOPMENT INDIA LTD. UTTAR PRADESH -892 \n  MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION     \n14 AIR INDIA ENGINEERING SERVICES LTD. DELHI -40710 \n15 AIR INDIA LTD. DELHI -383678 \n16 AIRLINE ALLIED SERVICES LTD. DELHI -19875 \n17 HOTEL CORPN. OF INDIA LTD. MAHARASHTRA -5910 \n  MINISTRY OF COAL     \n18 NLC TAMIL NADU POWER LTD. TAMILNADU -16003 \n  DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE     \n19 P E C LTD. DELHI -114202 \n20 STCL LTD. KARNATAKA -48007 \n  DEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS     \n21 BHARAT BROADBAND NETWORK LTD