HIGHWAYS  \n \nlM+d ifjogu vkSj jktekxZ ea=h \n \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether bursting of tyres is causing bus accidents especially in hilly areas as a result of \nwhich uncontrolled buses fall in gorges; \n(b) if  so, whether the Government proposes to make tubeless tyres compulsory in all \npassenger buses in the country to reduce such accidents; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE       \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n \n(a)  to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( d) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 346 ANSWERED ON 10TH AUGUST, 2017 ASKED BY DR. \nRAVINDRA KUMAR RAY REGARDING TUBELESS TYRES IN BUSES \n___________________________________________________________________________ \n \n(a) The Ministry of Road Transport and Highways collects data on road accidents from \nall States/UTs in a format developed as per the Asia Pacific Road Accident Database \n(APRAD) project of United Nations Economic and Social Commission for Asia Pacific \n(UNESCAP) region.  Under this format, accidents due to bursting of tyres are not collected.   \nRoad accidents are caused due to the  complex interaction of a number of factors. \nThese include driver’s fault, mechanical defects in the vehicles including bursting of tyres, \nfault of pedestrians,  road engineering and design defects,  bad weather, increase in vehicular \npopulation, etc.  It may not be possible to pin point any one reason for road accidents. \n(b) to (d)  No such proposal is under consideration in this Ministry. \n \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|STARRED   |347", "t": "Integrated Handloom Development Scheme", "head": "��������� �\n���������������������� ����\n�������������������� ���������\n�\n�������������������������������������� �\n�\n���������������������� ������������������ �\n������