 of \nthe country in the near future;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether this initiative will help in speedy and transparent \ntrial/disposal of the pending cases in various courts of the \ncountry; \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the time by which the Government is likely to implement \nthe  said system?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF LAW & JUSTICE AND \nELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n(a)  to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n2 \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 154 FOR 26TH JULY, 2017 \n \nREGARDING eCOURT SYSTEM \n \n(a) & (b):  The Government of India has an eCourt  project as a Mission \nMode Project for computerization of all the district and subordinate courts. \nKey features of the Project include delivery of the services; inter alia, case \nregistration, cause lists, daily case status, and final order/judgment. Further, \ne-filing of cases, e-payment of court fees, process service through email and \nalso through process servers having hand held devices, receipt of digitally \nsigned copies of judgments are key services.  \nAll the district and subordinate courts computerized under eCourts project \nhave been linked to National Judicial Data Grid (NJDG), which is a common \nrepository of case records across the country. The portal also provides online \ninformation to citizens including litigants such as details of case registration, \ncause list, case status, daily orders, and final judgments. Currently litigants \ncan access case status information in respect of over 7 crore pending and \ndecided cases and mo re than 4 crore orders / judgements pertaining to the \ncomputerized district and subordinate courts. As per information received \nfrom eCommittee of Supreme Court of India, funds have been released to \n16089 courts till date for computerization.  The High Cour t wise details of \nsuch district and subordinate courts are as under: \nS. \nNo. \nName of the High Court  No. of computerised courts  \n1.  Allahabad 1733 \n2.  Andhra Pradesh 1078 \n3.  Bombay 2079 \n4.  Calcutta 772 \n5.  Chattisgarh 340 \n6.  Delhi 427 \n7.  Gauhati 442 \n8.  Gujarat 1108 \n9.  Himachal Pradesh 118 \n\n3 \n \nS. \nNo. \nName of the High Court  No. of computerised courts  \n10.  Jabalpur 1203 \n11.  Jammu And Kashmir 218 \n12.  Jharkhand  m 351 \n13.  Jodhpur 978 \n14.  Karnataka 897 \n15.  Kerala 486 \n16.  Madras 988 \n17.  Orissa 509 \n18.  Patna 1025 \n19.  Punjab And Haryana 1018 \n20.  Sikkim 15 \n21.  Uttarakhand 185 \n22.  Tripura 62 \n23.  Manipur 30 \n24.  Meghalaya 27 \n Total 16089 \n \n(c) to (e): The eCourts Project equips the Judiciary to use ICT enabled tools \nto improve court and case management . The availability of online database \nenables judiciary to plan court administration. It leads to making the justice \ndelivery system across the country more transparent, more accessible and \naffordable. Availability of case data online imparts transparency to the \nfunctioning of courts and facilitates easy access of such data to lawyers and \nlitigants. T hus, ICT enablement of courts increases efficiency of justice \ndelivery system through organised and easy access to case information, and \ntransparency is the eventual outcome.  The eCourts Mission Mode Project is \nscheduled to culminate in 2019.  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |155", "t": "Female Labour Participation Rate", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 155 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.07.2017 \n \nFEMALE LABOUR PARTICIPATION RATE \n155. SHRI ABHISHEK SINGH: \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMEN T be pleased to \nstate:  \n(a)the details of the steps taken/being taken by the Government to \ncheck wage disparity among male and female labour force in the \ncountry; \n(b)whether the Government has taken any steps to improve and \nincrease female labour participation rate in the country and i f so, \nthe details thereof; \n(c)whether the Government has conducted any study in this regard;   \nand \n(d)if so, the details and the findings thereof along with the follow -up \naction taken thereon? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BANDARU DATTATREYA) \n \n(a) to (d):   The Equal Remuneration Act, 1976 provides for \npayment of  equal remuneration to men and women workers for same \nwork or work of similar nature without any discrimination. In the \nCentral sphere, the enforcement of the Act is entrusted to the Chief \nLabour Co mmissioner (Central) who heads the Central Industrial \nRelations Machinery (CIRM). Further, under the provisions of the \nMinimum Wages Act, 1948, the wages fixed by the appropriate \nGovernment are equally applicable to both male and female workers \nand the Act does not discriminate on the basis of gender. \n \nContd..2/- \n\n::  2  :: \n \n        Central Government has taken some prominent steps to \nincrease female labour participation rate which includes the \nenactment of the Maternity Benefit (Amendment) Act, 2017 which \nprovides for enhancement in paid maternity leave from 12 weeks to 26 \nweeks and provisions for mandatory crèche facility in the \nestablishments having 50 or more employees; issue of an advisory to \nthe States under the Factories Act, 1948 for permitting women \nworkers in the night shifts with adequate safety measures. Further, in \norder to enhance the employability of female workers, the Government \nis providing training to them through a network of Women Industrial \nTraining institutes, National Vocational Trainin g Institutes and \nRegional Vocational Training Institutes. A number of protective \nprovisions have been incorporated in various labour laws for creating \ncongenial work environment for women workers. \n \n     As per 5 th Annual Employment-Unemployment (EUS) Surve y \n(2015-16) conducted by Labour Bureau , the  Female Labour \nParticipation Rate by adopting Usual Principal Status (UPS) Approach \nis 23.7 and by Usual Principal and Subsidiary Status (UPSS) Approach \nis 27.4. \n \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |156", "t": "Vacancies of Principals in Kendriya Vidyalayas", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 156 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.07.2017 \n \nVACANCIES OF PRINCIPALS IN KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS \n \n156. SHRI Y.V. SUBBA REDDY:  \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESO URCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to \nstate:  \n(a)  whether it is a fact that many Kendriya Vidyalayas in the country, including KV \nin Ongole, Andhra Pradesh, do not have Principals for more than one year \nand if so, the details thereof, State/ UT-wise; \n(b)  whether it has come to the notice of the Government that there are vacancies \nof teachers at various levels in KV, Ongole;  \n(c)  if so, the action taken by the Government to fill in the said vacancies, \nincluding Principal, immediately;  \n(d)  the reasons for pendency regarding opening of KV at No. 2 Ongole and at \nRajampalli village of Yerragundlapale m in Prakasam district of Andhra \nPradesh in spite of these being reportedly cleared by KV Regional Office at \nHyderabad; and  \n(e)  by when the said proposal is likely to be cleared and start functioning?  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA) \n \n(a) No, Madam. Vacancy of Principal in Kendriya Vidyalaya (KV), Ongole is \nvacant with effect from 2.9.2016 only. However 118 KVs do not have Principal for \nmore than one year.  The State / UT-wise detail of KVs, where post of Principal is \nlying vacant for more than one year as on 1.6.2017, are annexed. \n(b) Yes, Madam. 34 posts of teachers on various levels are lying vacant in KV, \nOngole. \n  \n\n \n(c) Process of recruitment of 6115 teachers has been completed and final results \nare expected by July, 2017.  Thereafter, KVS will provide regular teachers on priority \nbasis against vacancies. The offer of appointment for 84 post of Principal has \nalready been issued and they are undergoing Induction course at Delhi. On \ncompletion of the course they will be posted against the vacant posts of Principal in \nvarious KVs including KV, Ongole. \n(d) The Government of India on 21.03.2017 has sanctioned 50 new Kendriya \nVidyalayas under Civil Sector. These Kendriya Vidyalayas are to be opened under \nchallenge mode i.e. on “First -cum-first served” basis. In accordance with the \ndecision of Government of India the orders for opening of the new Kendriya \nVidyalaya are to  be issued only after transfer of requisite extent of land in favour of \nKendriya Vidyalaya concerned.   In the matter of proposal for opening of new \nKendriya Vidyalayas at  KV, No.2 Ongole, District Prakasam and at Rajampalli, \nYerragondpallem, District Prakasam, the requisite extent of land in both the cases \nhas already been accepted by KVS. However, land transfer formalities have not \nbeen done so far by the state govt. \n(e) No definite time frame can be given as the State Government of Andhra \nPradesh has to complete land transfer formalities. \n***** \n  \n\nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PA RT (a) OF THE LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 156 RAISED BY SHRI Y. V. SUBBA REDDY, HON’BLE MEMBER OF \nPARLIAMENT TO BE ANSWERED ON 17 TH JULY 2017 REGARDING VACANCIES OF \nPRINCIPALS IN KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS. \n \nThe State /UT /KV-wise details of Kendriya Vidyalayas without permanent Principal for \nmore than one year as on 01.06.2017. \nSr. No. Name of State/UT Name of K V\n1 Arunachal Pradesh Dirang \n2 Andman & Nicobar No.2 Port Blair \n3 Assam AFS Kumbhirgram \n4 Assam Haflong \n5 Assam Misa Cantt. \n6 Assam Tamulpur \n7 Bihar Araria \n8 Bihar Banka \n9 Bihar CWP Bela \n10 Bihar Darbhanga No.2 \n11 Bihar Hajipur \n12 Bihar Harnaut \n13 Bihar Maharajganj\n14 Bihar CRPF Jhaphan \n15 Chhatisgarh Jagdalpur \n16 Chhatisgarh Kanker \n17 Chhatisgarh Malanjkhand \n18 Chhatisgarh Manendragarh \n19 Chhatisgarh Rajnandgaon \n20 Gujarat Bhavnagarpara \n21 Gujarat Bhuj Cantt \n22 Gujarat Dantiwada \n23 Gujarat Dwarka \n24 Gujarat Rly. Gandhidham \n25 Gujarat Himmatnagar \n26 Gujarat Viramgam \n27 Gujarat Godhra \n28 Gujarat Dahod\n29 Gujarat IFFCO Gandhidham \n30 Himachal Pradesh Naleti \n31 Himachal Pradesh Ghumarwin \n32 Himachal Pradesh Nadaun \n33 Himachal Pradesh Bakloh \n34 Jammu & Kashmir Anantnag \n35 Jammu & Kashmir Kargil \n36 Jammu & Kashmir Leh \n37 Jammu & Kashmir Rajouri (BSF) \n38 Jammu & Kashmir Humhama, BSF \n39 Jharkhand Garhwa \n40 Jharkhand Madhupur \n41 Jharkhand Simdega \n42 Karnataka Hassan \n43 Karnataka Hebbal\n44 Karnataka Koppal \n45 Karnataka No.1 Mangaluru \n46 Kerala NO. 1 CPCRI, Kasargod \n47 Kerala CRPF, Peringome \n48 Kerala Thalassery \n49 Kerala Kalpetta \n50 Kerala NTPC Kayamkulam \n51 Madhya Pradesh Datia \n52 Madhya Pradesh Mourena \n53 Madhya Pradesh Betul \n54 Madhya Pradesh Burhanpur \n55 Madhya Pradesh Guna \n56 Madhya Pradesh Harda \n\n57 Madhya Pradesh Hoshangabad \n58 Madhya Pradesh Nepanagar\n59 Madhya Pradesh Ratlam \n60 Madhya Pradesh ITBP Shivpuri \n61 Madhya Pradesh Tikamgarh \n62 Madhya Pradesh Chaurai\n63 Madhya Pradesh Dindori \n64 Madhya Pradesh Umaria \n65 Maharashtra Ganeshkhi"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |157", "t": "Funds Sanctioned for Cultural Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE \nLEGISLATIVE DEPARTMENT \n \nL O K     S A B H A \n \nSTARRED QUESTION No. *157 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 26TH  JULY, 2017. \n \nCompulsory Registration of Marriage \n \n*157. SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI: \n SHRI T. RADHAKRISHNAN:  \n  \n Will the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Union Government plans to make registration of \nmarriage compulsory and if so, details thereof and the reasons \ntherefor; \n(b) whether the Law Commission has given any recom mendations \nin this regard and if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Law Commission has recommended for \namendments in the Registration of Births and Deaths Act, 1969 \nfor the purpose instead of amending marriage laws and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n(d) whether the Supreme Court of India had made registration of \nmarriage compulsory through its judgement in 2006 and if so, \nthe details thereof; and \n(e) whether the Union Government also plans to link mandatorily \nsuch registration with Aadhar, if so, the details thereof  and the \nreasons therefor along with the action taken by the Union/State \nGovernments in this regard? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF LAW AND JUSTICE AND  \nELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n (a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 157 DUE FOR ANSWER \nON 26/07/2017. \n \n(a) In order to provide evidentiary value in matrimonial and \nmaintenance cases and prevent unnecessary harassment \nmeted out to women, it has been decided to provide for \ncompulsory registration of marriages. \n \n(b) and (c)  The 21 st Law Commission of India in its 270 th Report has, \ninter alia, recommended that the Registration of Births and \nDeaths Act, 1969 (18 of 1969) may be suitably amended to \ninclude registration of marriage within its scope so that the \nexisting administrative machinery would also be able to \ncarry out registration of marria ges.  The full Report of the            \nCommission is accessible from the website \nhttp://lawcommissionofindia.nic.in/reports/Report270.pdf \n \n(d)  The Supreme Court vide its judgment dated the 14 th \nFebruary, 2006 in the case of Seema Vs. Ashwani Kumar \nreported in A.I.R. 2006 SC 1158 had, inter alia,  directed \nthat marriages of all persons who are citizens of India \nbelonging to various religions should be made compulsorily \nregisterable in their respective States, where the marriages \nare solemnized. \n \n(e)  At pre sent, there is no proposal to link Aadhar for \nregistration of marriages. \n \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |158", "t": "Teachers Training", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 158 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.07.2017 \n \n \nTeachers Training \n \n158. SHRI P.K. BIJU: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is planning to open technical teachers training institutes in all the \nStates in the country including Kerala on th e lines of National Institute of Technical \nTeachers Training and Research (NITTTR) wherei n there would be facility of practical \ntraining; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/Union Territory-wise; and  \n(c) the time by when these institutes are likely to  be established to meet the increasing demand \nof such training?  \n \n \nANSWER  \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE \nDEVELOPMENT \n(DR. MAHENDRA NATH PANDEY) \n \n \n \n(a):     No, Madam.   \n \n(b) & (c):      Do not arise. \n \n \n \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |159", "t": "NGOs in Bihar", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 159 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.07.2017 \n \nNGOs in Bihar \n \n†159.    SHRIMATI RAMA DEVI: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the Non-Governmental Organizations (NGOs) in Bihar who have \nreceived financial assistance  for the on going schemes from the Ministry of Human \nResource Development during the last three years NGO-wise; \n \n(b) the names of the schemes under which the said NGOs have been provided financial \nassistance along with the amount sanctioned; and \n \n(c) the names of the NGOs out of the said NGOs found involved in irregularities? \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \nHUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA) \n(a) & (b): The details of the Non-Governmental Organizations (NGOs) in Bihar who \nhave received financial assistance for the ongoing Schemes from the Ministry of \nHuman Resource Development under the “Scheme of Support to Voluntary Agencies \nfor Adult Education and Skill Development” during the last three years NGO-wise are \nat Annexure. \n(c): No NGO, out of the aforesaid NGOs, is found involved in irregularities.  \n*****  \n\n \nAnnexure \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 159 FOR 17.07.2017 ASKED BY SHRIMATI RAMA \nDEVI REGARDING \"NGOs IN BIHAR\" \nFund sanctioned/released to NGOs in Bihar under the Scheme of Support to \nVoluntary Agencies for Adult Education and Skill Development during the last three \nyears \nS. \nNo. Name of the NGOs Amount sanctioned/released \n2014-15  2015-16   2016-17 \n  State Resource Centres     \n1 Asian Development Research \nInstitute (ADRI) Patna 41,97,087 98,20,443 1,12,04,763\n2 Deepayatan, Patna 50,00,000 1,14,34,215 1,25,65,306\n  Jan Shikshan Sansthans     \n3 Arwal 6,30,841 0 56,40,381\n4 Aurangabad 21,20,307 29,20,492 34,40,701\n5 Buxar 14,31,051 35,68,140 29,35,000\n6 Gaya 21,29,916 22,28,140 39,37,463\n7 Hajipur (Vaishali) 14,99,600 37,08,101 28,08,000\n8 Kishanganj 20,96,781 30,59,641 34,39,239\n9 Motihari 21,30,441 30,72,942 34,32,389\n10 Munger 15,00,000 33,52,740 34,25,330\n11 Muzaffarpur 21,30,741 30,62,993 34,14,113\n12 Nalanda 21,30,841 30,64,175 34,35,610\n13 Patna 20,54,980 15,00,000 42,45,651\n14 Samastipur 19,89,905 15,00,000 47,43,312\n15 Saran 21,30,441 30,49,139 28,44,159\n  Total 3,31,72,932 5,53,41,161 7,15,11,417\n \n******** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |16", "t": "Rashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 16 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.07.2017 \n \nRashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan \n \n \n16.  SHRI NISHIKANT DUBEY:  \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether funds from Centrally Sponsored Scheme of Rashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha \nAbhiyan, are provided to all States/UTs in cluding Jharkhand for providing infrastructure \nfacilities to Government secondary schools; and  \n \n(b) if so, the total amount allocated this year in the budget, State/UT-wise?  \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE \nDEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA) \n \n(a) & (b) : Yes. The budget provision for Rashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan for the \ncurrent financial year 2017-18 is Rs. 3830 crore. However, the funds are not allocated in the \nbudget State/UT-wise.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |160", "t": "BIS Mark for Electrical Transformers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 160\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.07.2017\n \n \nBIS MARK FOR ELECTRICAL TRANSFORMERS\n \n \n160\n.\n \n  \nSRIMTI RAKSHATAI KHADSE\n:\n \n \n(a)\n \nw\nhether Government proposes to make it \nmandatory for all manufacturer \n(Micro, Small & Medium) electrical transformers for distribution of electrical \npower to compulsorily undergo BIS Certification and if so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(b)\n \nw\nhether the Government has received memorandum to reconsider \nthis \namendment of compulsory BIS mark for the electrical transformers from \nvarious state organizations specially from Micro, Small and Medium Scale \nTransformer Manufacturing Associations; and\n \n \n(c)\n \ni\nf so, the details thereof and corrective steps being taken\n \nby the \nGovernment in this regards?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF\n \nMICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI HARIBHAI PAR\nA\nTHIBHAI CHAUDHARY)\n \n \n(a)\n:\n   \nYes.\n \n \nDepartment of Heavy Industries has notified Gazette notification no. \n948 dated  07.05.2015 titled as ‘Electrical Transformers (Quality Control) order, \n2015, making BIS certification mandatory for manufacturing of distribution \ntransformers upto 2500kVA rat\ning;\n \n \n(b)\n:\n  \nYes;\n \n \n(c)\n:\n  \nOne Representation from All India Transformer Manufacturers Association \n(AITMA), New Delhi has been received by this Ministry, wherein, it was \nrequested for making BIS Certification voluntary for the units having labeling\n \nlicense from BEE and vice versa. BIS is the national body for standard \nformulation therefore the matter was forwarded to BIS for necessary action.\n \n \n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |161", "t": "Formulation of Courses for Employment Generation", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n                MINISTRY OF SHIPPING \n \n            LOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.161 \n             TO BE ANSWERED ON 27th JULY, 2017 \n       INLAND WATERWAYS  \n*161.  SHRI S.R. VIJAYAKUMAR:  \nSHRI GAJANAN KIRTIKAR:  \n \nWill the Minister of SHIPPING be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details and the present implementation status of the Inland \nVessels (Prevention and Control of Pollution and Protection of \nInland Water) Rules, 2016 in the country;  \n \n(b)  the details of the projects under execution for expeditious \ntransportation of cargo and passengers and development of \ntourism through inland waterways across the country;  \n(c)  whether the Government has worked out a plan to encourage the \nuse of waterways for being more economical, customer friendly and \nsmooth movement of goods and passengers and if so, the details \nthereof;  \n(d)  the steps proposed to be taken by the Government to boost \nshipping tourism in the country particularly from Mumbai to \nElephanta Cave, Kolkata to Ganga Sagar and Dwarka Section; and  \n(e)  the steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government for \nmodernization of major Ports for easy and smooth movement of \ngoods? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF SHIPPING \n(SHRI NITIN GADKARI) \n \n    (a)to(e):   A Statement is laid on the table of the House.  \n \n \n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (e) of LokSabha Starred Question \nno.161  for 27.07.2017 raised by Shri S. R. Vijayakumar& Shri GajananKirtikar, \nM.Ps on “Inland  Waterways” \n \n(a):    Under the Inland Vessels Act, 1917, t he Inland Vessels (Prevention and \nControl of Pollution of Inland Water) Rules, 2016 were notified in July, 2016 \nand laid on the table of both the houses of Parliament. These rules are \nimplemented by the State Governments.  \n \n(b): Some of  the important projects taken up for expeditious transportation of \ncargo, passengers and development of tourism through inland waterways \nacross the country are as under:- \n \ni) Construction of multi -modal terminals at Varanasi, Sahibganj and \nHaldia; navigational lock at Farraka under the Jal Marg Vikas Project \non National Waterway -I to enable movement of inland vessels of \n1500-2000 tonnes capacity.  \nii) Roll-on Roll-off (Ro-Ro) services between Dhubri and Hatsingimari on \nNW-2 (River Brahmaputra) was operationalized during 2016 to reduce \nthe circuitous road route of about 220 km to reach Hatsingimarivia \nJogighopa.  \niii) Construction of Slipway at Pandu (Assam) on NW -2 which will be the \nfirst dry dock repair facility in the NE Region.  \niv) On National Waterway -3 (West Coast Canal from Kottapuram to \nKollam along with Champakara&Udyogmandal canal), two terminals \nat Bolghatty&Willingdon island have been made operational in Cochin \nPort Trust area for container traffic of  International Container \nTranshipment Terminal at Vallarpadam   (ICTT) through Ro -Ro / Low-\non Low-off (Lo-Lo) service. \nv) On NW -4, development of fairway between Muktyala to Vijayawada \nwhich will provide an efficient logistics solution to boost economic \ngrowth of the region and facilitate the development of Amravati, the \ncapital city of Andhra Pradesh as substantial construction material is \nexpected to be transported on this stretch. \nvi) A number of projects under SagarmalaProgramme for creation of \ninfrastructure for movement of  cargo/ passenger by sea/ National \nWaterways, have been sanctioned. The details of projects sanctioned \nare given at Annexure-I.  \n \n(c):     Under the National Waterways Act, 2016, 111 National Waterways (NWs) \nincluding the five existing NWs, have been declared. While NWs -1, 2 and 3 \nare operational and cargo and passenger vessels are moving on them, on \nNW-4, the development works under Phase-I have been sanctioned in 2017.  \nFurther, preparation of a  Detailed Project Report (DPR) including Front End \nEngineering Design (FEED) for the construction of weirs / barrages with \nnavigational locks and check dams between Pankapal and Paradip / Dhamra \n(Phase –I) has been sanctioned for development of NW-5. \n \nOut of 106 newly declared NWs, 32 NWs are considered technically \nfeasible so far.  The list of these 32 NWs is at Annexure-II.  Out of these 32 \nNWs the development of 7 NWs has been approved as per details at \nAnnexure-III. \n\n \n \n(d):The development and promotion of tourists’ destinations including cruise circuits and \nimplementation of tourism projects is primarily the responsibility of the \nrespective State Government/Union Territory Administration.   \n \n        The State Government of Gujarat has informed that to boost shipping \ntourism in Dwarka section, under its Tourism Policy of 2015, it is promoting cruise \nterminals at Okha, Dwarka, Porbandar and Veraval. The Maharashtra Maritime \nBoard has informed that to facilitate to and fro passengers movement between \nMumbai and Elephanta Caves, installation of a new floating pantoon at \nShetbunder, Elephanta Island and work for widening and increasing the height of \nthe existin"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |162", "t": "India as Global Hotspot in Tourism", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†162 \nANSWERED ON 17.07.2017 \n \nINDIA AS GLOBAL HOTSPOT IN TOURISM  \n \n†162. SHRIMATI RANJANBEN BHATT:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether it is a fact that the Government is considering to work \non a large scale for transforming the country in to a global \nhotspot in tourism;  \n(b)  whether the Government has taken any steps in this direction \ntill date; and  \n(c)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons  therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA) \n \n(a) to (c): The Ministry of Tourism is working on following three \npronged strategy for developing India as a Global Tourism \ndestination: \n \n1. Development of infrastructure \n2. Creating tourist friendly environment  \n3. Promotion and Publicity in the overseas and domestic \nmarkets \n \nTo meet the requirements of infrastructural gaps at tourism \ndestinations, the Ministry has launched two schemes in 2014 -15 i.e. \nSwadesh Darshan - Integrated Development of Theme -Based Tourist \nCircuits and PRASAD - Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual \nAugmentation Drive. The Ministry has  sanctioned 63 projects for \nRs.5309.95 crore under Swadesh Darshan  scheme and 19 projects \nfor Rs.529.12 crore under PRASAD scheme as on 30.06.2017.  \n\nFor creating conducive environment for tourists the Ministry \nhas implemented facilities of E -Visa, WiFi connectivity at tourist \ndestinations, Web based public delivery system, 24X7 helpline \nservice, Safety and Security, Cleanliness at the tourist destinations.  \n \nFor creating awareness about the tourism products of the \ncountry, the Ministry is promoting India as a holistic destination as \npart of its on -going activities, releases print, electronic, online and \noutdoor media c ampaigns in the domestic and international \nmarkets, under the Incredible India brand -line.  The Ministry also \npromotes the tourism destinations and products through its \nwebsites and publicity and promotional material produced by it from \ntime to time. \n \n********* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |163", "t": "Inclusion of Monuments in the Protected List", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.163\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.7.2017\n \nASHADHA 26, 1939 (SAKA)\n \n \n \nINCLUSION OF MONUMENTS IN THE PROTECTED LIST\n \n \n \n163.\n \nSHRI NISHIKANT DUBEY:  \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to s\ntate: \n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether Government proposes to include certain monuments/heritage sites of \nthe country in the list of monuments of national importance/protected \nmonuments; and \n \n \n(b)\n \nif so, the details thereof, monument\n-\nwise and heritage, site\n-\nwise in Jharkhan\nd as \nwell as in other States of the country, State/UT\n-\nwise?\n \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE, CULTURE AND TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)\n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n \n(a)&(b)\n \nYes, Madam. The list of monuments identified for protection as of national \nimportance (Stat\ne\n-\nwise including Jharkhand) is at Annexure.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE \n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a)&(b) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 163 FOR 17.7.2017\n \nLIST OF MONUMENTS IDENTIFIED FOR PROTECTION AS OF NATIONAL \nIMPORTANCE IN THE COUNTRY \n \nSl\n.No\n \nName of Monument\n \nState\n \n \n1\n \nBadarpur Fort, District Karimganj\n \nAssam\n \n2\n \nRock Painting at Chintakunta Muddanur \nMandal, District Y.S.R. Kadapa\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n3\n \nAncient Site, Khirsara, District Kacchh\n \nGujarat\n \n4\n \nBudhist Monastery at Lahaling District Lalau\nl \nand Spiti.\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n5\n \nZimskhang Gongma along with village remains\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n6\n \nAncient Caves Saspol/Gon\n-\nNila\n-\nPhuk Meditition \nCaves Saspol, Leh\n-\nLaadakh \n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n7\n \nRock Art site Murgi, Leh\n-\nLaadakh\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n8\n \nTrilochana Templ\ne\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n9\n \nTrisher Stupa along with adjusting remains and \nprehistoric site around Holy Lake (Slob Phan \nRso), Leh\n-\nLaadakh\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n10\n \nNavratana Garh Fort \n \nJharkahand\n \n11\n \nJanardana Temple, District Thrissur\n \nKerala\n \n12\n \nVishnu Temple (Visnugu\ndi) Nadavyal \nSultanbatery Taluk, Wayanad, District Thrissur\n \nKerala\n \n13\n \nDhurabarao (Putalibarao)\n \nMaharashtra\n \n14\n \nMonument at Ranipur Jharial\n \nOdisha\n \n15\n \nZawar, Zinc Protection Centre\n \nRajasthan\n \n16\n \nThe four Mughal Gardens \n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n17\n \nHaveli of Agah Kha\nn\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n18\n \nHaveli of Khan\n-\ni\n-\nDuran \n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n19\n \nHathi Khana\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n20\n \nSomeshwar Mahadeva Temple Char, District \nChitrakut.\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n21\n \nAncient Brick Temple (Tundare Baba), Deokalia \n(Unchagaon) in District Sitapur.\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n22\n \nSanauli\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |164", "t": "Auction of Oil and Gas Fields", "head": " \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 164 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17th July, 2017 \n \n Auction of Oil and Gas Fields \n \n \n164.   SHRI C.S. PUTTA RAJU: \n         \nपैट्रोलियम व प्राकृलिक गैस मंत्री  \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to go for auction of oil and gas fields in the country and if so, \nthe details thereof along with number of such blocks offered at present, company/block-wise;  \n \n \n(b) whether the last New Exploration Licensing Policy (NELP) has not been able to achieve the \nexploration target;  \n \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof along with action taken by the Government to restructure earlier \nhydrocarbon policy and to formulate new auction policy; and  \n \n(d) whether opinion of the various Public Sector Undertakings under the control of Ministry of \nPetroleum and Natural Gas and experts/consultants has been taken on the proposed auction \npolicy and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nपेट्रोलियम और प्राकृलिक गैस मंत्रािय में राज्य मंत्री (स्विन्त्त्र प्रभार) (श्री धमेन्त्र प्रधान) \nMINISTER OF STATE (I/C) IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI DHARAMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a). The Government of India has launched the new  Hydrocarbon Exploration & Licensing Policy \n(HELP) and Open Acreage Licensing Policy (OALP) on 1 st July, 2017. Under the Hydrocarbon \nExploration & Licensing Policy (HELP) bidder will have option for carving out exploratory blocks \nfrom the designat ed areas and submit the Expression of Interest (EOI). Thereafter, blocks will be \nawarded through international competitive bidding process. The number of blocks available for \nauction will depend upon on the Expression of Interest (EoI) received during init ial window that \nends on 15th November, 2017.  \n \n(b) & (c). The objective of the New Exploration Licensing Policy (NELP) was to attract the private \ninvestment in oil and gas sector to enhance domestic production.  Nine bidding rounds were held.  \nThe NELP policy was based on Production Sharing Contract where Government take depends on \nsharing of profit petroleum, based on Pre-Tax Investment Multiple (PTIM) after cost recovery which \nresulted in many arbitration /court cases. Government has launched  the new H ydrocarbon \nExploration Licensing Policy (HELP) and Open Acreage Licensing Policy (OALP) with the objective \nof boosting the production of oil and gas in the Indian Sedimentary Basins. The salient features of \nnew Hydrocarbon Exploration & Licensing Policy (HELP) are as follows: - \n \n\n \n \n \n \n Single License for conventional and unconventional Hydrocarbons.  \n An Open Acreage Licensing Policy. \n Easy to administer Revenue Sharing Model  \n Marketing & pricing freedom for the Crude oil & Natural gas produced. \n Zero royalty rates for deepwater and ultra deepwater blocks for first seven years. \n Equal weightage to work programme and fiscal share   \n No oil cess \n Custom duty exemption etc. \n \n(d). The Hydrocarbon Exploration & Licensing Policy (HELP)  was prepared after wide ranging \nconsultations with various stake holders such as public and private operators, service providers, \nindustry and trade associations etc., including  Public Section Undertakings under the jurisdiction \nof this Ministry.  \n                                                                  \n****** \n       "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |165", "t": "Displaced Tribals", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.165 \nANSWERED ON 27TH JULY 2017 \n \nUNDERPASSES ON NHS \n \n*165. SHRI ARVIND SAWANT: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसडक परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of underpasses on the National Highways (NHs) in the country, State-wise; \n(b) whether the Government proposes  to construct new underpasses while  constructing six \nlane National Highways; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the  number of underpasses constructed from  2010 till date, \nNH-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n (SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a)  to (c)  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 165 ANSWER ED ON 27.07.2017  ASKED BY SHRI ARVIND \nSAWANT REGARDING UNDERPASSES ON NHS. \n \n(a)  There are 5422 numbers of underpasses on the National Highways in the country \nincluding 949 numbers of underpasses on six lane NHs. \n(b) to (c)  Yes, Madam. Ministry proposes to construct new underpasses while constructing \nthe six lane National Highways . Most of the six-laning projects have been undertaken for \nconstruction after 2010 . The State -wise and NHs wise details of the underpasses including \nunderpasses on the six lane NHs are given at Annexure-I.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 165 ANSWER ED ON 27.07.2017  ASKED BY SHRI ARVIND SAWANT  \nREGARDING UNDERPASSES ON NHS. \n \nDetails of the underpasses on the National Highways \n \nSl. \nNo. \nState NH No. No. Of \nUnderpass \nNumber of \nUnderpasses \nconstructed/under \nconstruction on six \nlane  \n1 Andhra Pradesh 7, 214, 18, 5, 544D, 9 358 110 \n2 Assam 37, 54, 31, 31C, 36, 52, 53 51 0 \n3 Bihar 107, 19, 2, 28, 30, 327E, 57, 77, 83 81 63 \n4 Chhattisgarh 200, 130C, 6 58 11 \n5 Goa 17, 17B, 4A 8   \n6 Gujarat 228, 1, 14, 15, 51, 56, 58, 59, 6, 68, 8, \n8A, 8B, 8C, 8D, 8E  701 166 \n7 Haryana 1, 10, 2, 22, 619E, 71, 71A, 8 271 39 \n8 Himachal Pradesh 1A 1   \n9 Jammu & Kashmir 144, 144A, 1A 17   \n10 Jharkhand 2, 31, 33 29 7 \n11 Karnataka 206, 207, 212, 13, 150A, 17, 275zz, 4, \n48, 7  779 52 \n12 Kerala 220, 17, 47, 966A 60 6 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 25, 26, 26B, 3, 59, 69, 69A, 7, 75, 86  223 1 \n14 Maharashtra 161, 211, 222, 361, 17, 222zz, 3, 4, 4B, \n4C, 50, 6, 69, 7, 753A, 8, 9 756 95 \n15 Manipur 39 2   \n16 Meghalaya 6 1   \n17   Delhi 24, 1 5   \n18 Odisha 55, 316, 16, 20, 26, 49, 53, 194 43 \n19 Punjab 1, 10, 15, 1A, 21, 22, 344B, 64, 95 171 71 \n20 Rajasthan 112, 113, 116, 11, 11A, 12, 14, 3, 65, \n76, 76B, 79, 79A, 8 389 62 \n21 Sikkim 310A 1   \n22 Tamil Nadu 226, 532, 4, 45, 45B, 45C, 46, 47, 5, 66, \n67,