hospitals are not functioning properly due to the shortage of\nfunds; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken/proposed to be taken by the\nGovernment for proper allocation of funds to these hospitals?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE(IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF AYURVEDA,\nYOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\nSTATEMENT REFFERED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 199 FOR 28TH JULY, 2017\n(a) The number of Ayurveda, Yoga and Naturopathy, Unani, Siddha and\nHomoeopathy (AYUSH) hospitals in the c ountry, State/UT -wise is enclosed as\nAnnexure-I.\n(b) and (c) Health is a State subject. Therefore, assessment of performance of these\nhospitals comes under the purview of respective State/UT Governments.\n(d) and (e) Health is a State subject . Functioning of Hospitals comes under the\npurview of State/UT Government. However, under Centrally Sponsored Scheme of\nNational AYUSH Mission (NAM), there is provision of financial assistance for\nupgrading AYUSH Hospitals and setting up of upto 50 bedded in tegrated AYUSH\nhospitals. The State/UT Governments are eligible to submit their proposal through\nthe State Annual Action Plan (SAAP) as per the guidelines of the scheme. Further,\nthe status of  grant -in-aid provided under the components of upgradation of AYUSH\nHospitals and setting up of upto 50 bedded integrated AYUSH hospitals during\n2016-17 is furnished at Annexure-II.\n\nAnnexure-I\nState wise/System wise Number of AYUSH Hospitals as on 1-4-2016\nS.\nNo\n.\nState / UT\nAyurveda Unani\nSiddh\na\nYog\na Naturopath\ny\nHomoeopath\ny\nSowa\n-\nRigp\na\nTotal\nA. States/Union Territories\n1 Andhra Pradesh 3 2 0 0 0 3 0 8\n2\nArunachal\nPradesh 10 0 0 0 0 2 0 12\n3 Assam 1 0 0 0 0 3 0 4\n4 Bihar 5 1 0 0 0 2 0 8\n5 Chhattisgarh 9 1 0 1 0 3 0 14\n6 Delhi 2 1 0 0 0 2 0 5\n7 Goa 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0\n8 Gujarat 42 0 0 0 6 16 0 64\n9 Haryana 9 1 0 0 0 1 0 11\n10 H