nsumers and \nuninterrupted powe r \nis being supplied to \nagriculture sector. \nJammu & \nKashmir* \n- - - - \nPunjab 04:00 - 07:44 \nhrs/day     \n04:54 - 09:14 \nhrs/day     \n06:13 - 10:51 \nhrs/day    \n06:09 - 09:01 hrs/day  \nRajasthan 06.45 hrs/day               06.45 hrs/day               06.45 hrs/day               06:30 - 06.45 hrs/day \nUttar Pradesh 18:44 - 19:47 \nhrs/day  \n13:07 - 19:05 \nhrs/day  \n09:38 - 13:38 \nhrs/day \n07:02 - 15:48 hrs/day \nUttarakhand 22:42 - 24:00  \nhrs/day     \n23:13 - 24:00  \nhrs/day     \n19:31 -  23:55 \nhrs/day     \n18:29 -  24:00 \nhrs/day     \nWestern Region          \nChhattisgarh 18 hrs/day                                                    18 hrs/day                                                    18 hrs/day                                                    18 hrs/day                                                    \nGujarat 8 hours/day. 8 hours/day. 8 hours/day. 8 hours/day. \nMadhya Pradesh 3  phase \n(irrigartion): 9.31- \n09:46 hrs /day           \n3 phase (Mixed) \nSupply:  23:00 - \n23:16 hrs /day      \n 3 phase (Mixed) \nSupply: 22:53 - \n23:25 hrs /day      \n3 phase (Mixed) \nSupply: 22:51 - \n23:32 hrs /day      \n 3 phase (Mixed) \nSupply:21:34 - 23:35 \nhrs /day      \nMaharashtra 9 hrs/day                          9 -12 hrs/day                          9 hrs/day 9 hrs/day  \nGoa No restriction No restriction No restriction No restriction \nSouthern Region         \nAndhra Pradesh 07 hrs/d"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |866", "t": "Works Undertaken under MGNREGS", "head": "O.I.H.\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 866\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2017\nWORKS UNDERTAKEN UNDER MGNREGS\n866. SHRI SHYAMA CHARAN GUPTA:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of amount spent under Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment\nGuarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) in Allahabad region in Uttar Pradesh during the last\nthree years;\n(b) whether a probe into works undertaken under MGNREGS in Baidvar Kala village in\nKoraon block in TransYamuna region can be made and actual position can be ascertained\nand provided in this regard; and\n(c) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a): Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) is\nimplemented by the State Government. As per State Government of Uttar Pradesh, the\nexpenditure in Allahabad region of Uttar Pradesh during last three financial years is as under:\n(Rs. in lakh)\nDistricts Expenditure as per MIS\n2014-15 2015-16 2016-17\nAllahabad 4189.47 4526.97 9388.345\nPratapgarh 6663.38 7019.03 10797.81\nKausambi 1093.35 1546.06 2578.94\nFatehpur 4180.2 5051.1 7516.07\n(b) & (c): State Government of Uttar Pradesh has stated that if any serious complaint   is\nreceived, a probe may be undertaken.\nThe details of works undertaken under MGNREGS by the State are made available on\nNREGASoft. The list of works undertaken in Baidvar kala Panchayat is at Annexure.\n*****\n\nAnnexure referred in reply to parts (b)&(c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question\nNo. 866 dated 20.07.2017.\nState: UTTAR PRADESH\nDistrict: ALLAHABAD\nBlock: KORAON\nPanchayat: Baidvar kala\nProgress FY 2016-17 FY 2015-16 FY 2014-15\nTotal Households Worked (In Nos) 60 81 24\nTotal Individuals Worked (In Nos) 82 112 28\nPersondays Generated so far (In Nos) 583 1,274 327\nAverage days of employment provided per Household 10 16 14\nTotal Expenditure (Rs. in Lakhs.) 1.94 0.52 1.67\nWages (Rs. In Lakhs) 1.94 0.52 0.55\nTotal No. of Works Takenup (New+Spill Over) 35 18 13\nNumber of Ongoing Works (In Nos) 26 12 13\nNumber of Completed Works (In Nos) 9 6 0"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |867", "t": "Promotion of Sports in NER", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.867 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2017 \nPromotion of Sports in NER \n867. SHRI RAM PRASAD SARMAH: \n Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to \nstate:  \n(a)  whether the Government has taken or proposes to take \nsteps for promoting the talented sportspersons/athletes from \nNorth Eastern Region (NER) to participate at National and \nInternational level;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the policy adopted by \nthe Government in this regard;  \n(c)  whether it is a fact that only limited number of \nsportspersons belonging to NER get opportunity to participate at \nInternational level due to sh ortage of sports infrastructure in that \nregion; and  \n(d)  if so, the remedial steps taken/ being taken by the \nGovernment in this regard? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR \nYOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI VIJAY GOEL) \n(a) & (b): Yes, Madam. To promote sports in the country including \nNorth Eastern Region, SAI is implementing the following sports \npromotional schemes to identify talented sports persons in the \nage groups ranging from 8 -25 years and nurture them to excel at \nnational and international competitions across the country: \n \n\n National Sports Talent Contest Scheme (NSTC) \n Army Boys Sports Company (ABSC) \n SAI Training Centre (STC) \n Special Area Games (SAG) \n Extension Centre of STC/SAG \n Centre of Excellence (CoE) \n National Sports Academies (NSA) \n \nFurther to strengthen the ongoing schemes of SAI in the \nNorth East ern Region, SAI has decided to operationalize  its SAG \nCentre at Churachandpur , Manipur and to identify more number of \nsports person s from North East,  SAI has decided to upgrade or \nestablish the following Centres in the North Eastern Region : \n \n Shifting of existing STC Shillong to North Eastern Hill University, \nShillong where international standard sports infrastructure has \nbeen created. \n Establishment of SAG Centre at Solal Gaon, North Lakhimpur, \nAssam \n Relocation of t he existing STC at Dimapur on land provided by \nthe State Government.   \n \n(c) & (d): No , Madam. As a matter of fact, considering the nature \nand topography of North Eastern States, large number of sports \nperson men and women in variou s sports discipline participate  in \nthe international level competitions and bring laurels for the \ncountry. \nMoreover, a  Scheme called “Khelo India – National \nProgramme for Development of Sports” i s being implemented by \nthis Ministry as a Central Sector Scheme from the financial year \n2016-17. This Scheme provides for creation of sports \ninfrastructure facilities, namely, Synthetic Athletic Track, \nSynthetic Hockey Field, Synthetic Turf Football Ground , \nMultipurpose hall, Swimming pool and construction of stadia \ncomplex, holding of sports competitions in two age groups of (i) \nunder 14 and (ii) under 17 all over India to encourage mass \nparticipation of both boys and girls in Sports in both urban and \nrural areas and identification of talented sportspersons, etc., all \nover the country including North Eastern Region (NER). \n*** \n\n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |869", "t": "Tenders for New Solar Energy Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 869 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2017 \n \nTENDERS FOR NEW SOLAR ENERGY PROJECTS \n \n869. SHRI UDAY PRATAP SINGH: \n \n Will the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the announcement of tenders of new solar energy projects and allocation of \nprojects have come down significantly; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR POWER, COAL, NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND \nMINES (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) & (b): Tenders of 2900 MW for ground mounted projects have been issued from April to \nJune 2017 (3 months) under  the Central and State schemes, while Tenders of 4835 MW projects \nwere issued during 2016 -17 under  the Central and State schemes.  The d etails are as given \nbelow: \nTotal Tenders issued during 2016-17 \nSI. \nNo \nOrganisations/States Capacity (MW) \n1 Central Scheme/CPSU 3510 \n2 Under State Scheme 1325 \n Total 4835 \nTotal Tenders issued during 2017-18 ( upto 30th June,2017) \nSI. \nNo \nOrganisations/States Capacity (MW) \n1 Central Scheme/CPSU 750 \n2 Under State Scheme 2150 \n3 Total 2900 \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |87", "t": "Field Offices of Rubber Board", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 87\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 21ST JULY, 2017\nVACANT POSTS IN GOVERNMENT MEDICAL COLLEGES\n*87. SHRIMATI KIRRON KHER:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether a number of posts of Professor, Nurse, Technician are lying vacant in all\nthe Government medical colleges across the country;\n(b) if so, the details thereof during the last t hree years, category-wise and State/ UT-\nwise including Punjab, Haryana and Chandigarh;\n(c) whether any plan has been formulated by the Government to fill up these vacant\nposts, if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and\n(d) the other corrective steps taken by the Government in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (d) : A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 87* FOR 21ST JULY, 2017\n(a) to (d) Health being a State subject, the responsibility for filling up of vacant posts\nin Government medical colleges is under the jurisdiction of respective State\nGovernments.  The data pertaining to vacan t posts of Professor, Nurses and\nTechnicians in medical colleges of State Governments is not maintained centrally.\nHowever, the information regarding vacant posts in Central Government Institutions\nis at Annexure.\nTo increase the availability of faculty in medical colleges in the country,\nCentral Government has taken several steps. These include:\ni) The ratio of teachers to students has been revised from 1:1 to 1:2 for all\nMD/MS disciplines and 1:1 to 1:3 in subjects of Anaesthesiology, Forensic\nMedicine, Radi otherapy, Medical Oncology, Surgical Oncology and Psychiatry.\nFurther, teacher: student ratio in public funded Government Medical Colleges for\nProfessor has been increased from 1:2 to 1:3 in all clinical subjects and for Asso.\nProf. from 1:1 to 1:2 if the Asso. Prof. is a unit head.\nii) DNB qualification has been recognized for appointment as faculty to take care\nof shortage of faculty.\niii) Enhancement of age limit for appointment/extension/re -employment against\nposts of teachers/dean/principal/ director in medical colleges from 65-70 years.\niv) Removal of embargo/rider imposed on foreign qualified PG Doctors from five\nEnglish speaking nations i.e. US, UK, Canada, Australia and New Zealand in case of\ntaking teaching profession. They are now allowed to take up the post of Assistant\nProfessor in respective department.\nv) Assured Career Progression Scheme for faculty of Central Government\nInstitutions has been revised to make it more beneficial.\nvi) Various allowances available to faculty like Non Practicing Allowance,\nConveyance Allowance, Learning Resource Allowance, etc. have been enhanced\nconsiderably.\nvii) The Central Government is implementing a Centrally Sponsored Scheme for\nincrease of PG seats in Government Medical Colleges.\n\nAnnexure\nJawaharlal Institute of Postgraduate Medical Education and Research (JIPMER), Puducherry\nSr.\nNo.\nName of the Post Number of\nVacancies\n2014-15\nNumber of\nVacancies\n2015-16\nNumber of\nVacancies\n2016-17\n1. Professor 22 23 0\n2. Staff  Nurse 40 40 82\n3. Laboratory Technician 48 13 21\nNorth Eastern Indira Gandhi Regional Institute of Health & Medical Sciences (NEIGRIHMS), Shillong\nSr.\nNo.\nName of the Post Number of\nVacancies\n2015\nNumber of\nVacancies\n2016\nNumber of\nVacancies\n2017 (as on\n17.07.2017)\n1. Professor 65 61 44\n2. Staff  Nurse 26 23 04\n3. Technician 88 59 53\nTeaching Specialist Sub-Cadre of Central Health Service (CHS)\nSr.\nNo.\nInstitute Number of\nVacancies\n2015\nNumber of\nVacancies\n2016\nNumber of\nVacancies\n2017\n1. LHMC, New Delhi 73 62 64\n2. S.J. Hospital (including CIO) 49 211 194\n3. AIIH&PH, Kolkata 5 3 4\n4. CIP, Ranchi 32 32 21\n5. AIPMR, Mumbai 4 2 2\n6. RML Hospital, Delhi 72 40 34\n7. Total 235 350 319\n\nRecruitment status of Professor posts in AIIMS\nSr. No. Institute Sanctioned Employed Vacancies Advertised\n1 AIIMS Bhopal 54 8 46 46\n2 AIIMS Bhubaneswar 54 13 41 41\n3 AIIMS Jodhpur 54 14\n40\nBeing re-\nadvertised\n4 AIIMS Patna 54 8 46 46\n5 AIIMS Raipur 54 9 45 45\n6 AIIMS Rishikesh 54 14\n40\nJoining of 4\nawaited. 36 re-\nadvertised"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |870", "t": "Capacity Increase in Solar Power Plants", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 870 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2017 \n \nCAPACITY INCREASE IN SOLAR POWER PLANTS \n \n \n870. SHRI TARIQ ANWAR: \n \n \n Will the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n \n \n(a) the details of capacity increase in the solar power plants during the last three years, State-wise; \n \n(b) whether the Government has taken any concrete steps for use of solar energy by the Government, \ncorporates, hotels and hospitals; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether the Government has brought any scheme to encourage the use of solar energy in the country; \nand \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR POWER, COAL, NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND MINES \n(INDEPENDENT CHARGE) (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n \n(a): There has been a significant increase in solar power installations in last three years.  State -wise details \nof grid connected solar capacity installed during last three years are given at Annexure. \n \n(b) & (c): The Government has launched Grid Connected Rooftop and Small Solar Power Plants \nProgramme to promote use of solar energy among the residential, community, institutional, industrial and \ncommercial establishments.  Under this programme, 30% subsidy is p rovided to residential, institutional and \nsocial establishments in general category states and 70% subsidy is provided in the said sectors in special \ncategory states, Islands and NE states.  For Government institutions, achievement linked incentive is \nprovided and no subsidy is provided for commercial, industrial and private sectors. \n \n(d) & (e): The Government has launched following schemes to promote the use of solar energy in the \ncountry: \n \ni. Solar Park Scheme for setting up of over 25 Solar Parks and Ultra Mega Solar Power Projects \ntargeting over 20,000 MW capacity (capacity recently enhanced to 40,000 MW). \n \nii. Scheme for setting up 1000 MW of Grid -Connected Solar PV Power Projects by  the Central Public \nSector Undertakings (CPSUs) and various Government of India organisations with VGF. \n \niii. Scheme for setting up 300 MW of Grid -Connected Solar PV Power Projects by Defence \nEstablishments and Para Military Forces with VGF.  \n \niv. Pilot-cum-demonstration project for development of grid connected solar PV power plants on canal \nbanks and canal tops. \nv. Bundling Scheme - 15000 MW grid-connected solar PV power plants through NTPC Ltd./ NVVN. \nvi. VGF Scheme for setting up of 2000 MW of Grid Connected Solar PV Power Projects through SECI.  \nvii. VGF Scheme for setting up of 5000 MW of Grid Connected Solar PV Power Projects through SECI . \nviii. Installation of Grid Connected Solar Rooftop Power Plants. \n \n******* \n \n\nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA UN