ce the cost of electricity by ensuring more \nefficient fuel usage;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the Government may eventually extend the facility to other coal \nconsuming industries as well; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (I/C) IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL, POWER,  NEW &  \nRENEWABLE ENERGY AND MINES \n \n( SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL ) \n \n(a) to (d) : Based on the recommendation s of the Inter -Ministerial Task Force \n(IMTF), the swapping of coal supplies has been permitted between the units of public \npower producers only. The Flexibility in Coal Utilization Policy, issued by the Ministry \nof Power, allows use o f coal by any State/Central Generating Company in Private \nGenerating Station of Independent Power Producers (IPPs). Under case 4  of the \npolicy, the State/Central Generating Company supplying coal will invite Tariff Bids \nfrom prospective IPP Generating Stations for use of domestic coal out of aggregated \ncoal allocated to respective State/CGS and the IPP shall supply Power in lieu of \ntransfer of such coal. \n* * * * * \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |904", "t": "ODF Villages", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Drinking Water and Sanitation \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 904  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20-07-2017 \n \nODF Villages \n \n904. SHRI B. SRIRAMULU: \n \nWill the Minister of  DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION be pleased to state : \n(a) the details of villages which have been declare d as Open Defecation Free (ODF) during \nthe last three years, State/ UT-wise; \n \n(b) the amount spent to make these villages ODF dur ing the said period including Karnataka, \nState/UT-wise; and \n \n(c) the policy bottlenecks and implementation defic iencies noticed in making a village open \ndefecation free along with the remedial action taken thereon? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \n(SHRI RAMESH CHANDAPPA JIGAJINAGI) \n \n \n(a) The States/UTs - wise number of villages have b een declared as Open Defecation Free \n(ODF) during the last three year including the current financial year is at Annexure-I. \n \n(b) the amount spent to make these villages ODF dur ing the said period including Karnataka, \nState/UT-wise is at Annexure –II; and \n \n(c) Sanitation is a behavioral issue. It involves c hange of mindset amongst people to stop \nopen defecation and to adopt safe sanitation practi ces. This requires engagement of \ncommunity and skill in facilitation and ‘triggering ’. There is constraint of these capacities. \nFurther, the States having large population have a bigger task of becoming ODF as compared \nto smaller States. In order to address the above pr oblems, a number of measures are being \ntaken. These include:  \n• Focus on behavior change: Community based collectiv e behavior change has been \nmentioned as the preferred approach, although the S tates are free to choose the \napproach best suited to them.  \n• The programme provides flexibility to the States in  the implementation. This is \nessential, given the vast socio-economic-cultural d iversity of India, and also from the \npoint of view of promoting innovations.  \n• There is a greater emphasis on capacity building, especially in community approaches \nand programme management.  \n• The programme is being run as a janandolan  with cooperation of all sections of the \nsociety including the NGOs, Corporates, youth etc. The Panchayats are being actively \ninvolved. \n\n \n \n \nAnnexure-I. \nStatement referred in part (a) of the reply to Lok Sabha Un-starred Question No.904 \ndue for reply on 20-07-2017 \nState/ UT-wise , villages declared Open Defecation Free (ODF) during the last two \nyears and current financial year  \nS.N. State/UT 2015-2016 2016-2017 2017-2018 \n1 A & N Islands 0  0 3 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 380  3528  523  \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 206  1502  934  \n4 Assam  201  466  2714  \n5 Bihar  195  1727  335  \n6 Chhattisgar