DU 560.44 506.17 14.60 \nTELANGANA 157.53 174.02 74.30 \nTRIPURA 52.89 20.03 2.07 \nUTTAR PRADESH 571.91 1132.21 222.98 \nUTTARAKHAND 71.67 149.55 29.42 \nWEST BENGAL 904.79 838.60 131.24 \n  9370.47 10027.48 1454.11 \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |906", "t": "Phasing Out of Conventional Energy Subsidies", "head": " \n \n \n                                                                                                                                                                                                    \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 906 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2017 \n \nPHASING OUT OF CONVENTIONAL ENERGY SUBSIDIES  \n \n \n \n906. SHRI C. MAHENDRAN: \n \n \n Will the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n \n \n(a) whether the Government is planning to phase out all conventional energy subsidies in near \nfuture;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the Government is planning to force petroleum products to compete with other fuels like \nbiomass and biogas, etc.; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR POWER, COAL, NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND MINES \n(INDEPENDENT CHARGE) (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n \n(a) & (b): No Madam.  \n \n \n(c) & (d): According to  the National Policy on Biofuel, the indicative t arget for blending is 20% \nbio-fuel with petroleum fuel by the 2017. \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |907", "t": "Welfare of Textile Workers", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.907 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2017 \n \nWELFARE OF TEXTILE WORKERS \n \n907. COL. SONARAM CHOUDHARY: \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंरी   \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the incentives and other welfare schemes for textile workers being \nimplemented by the Government; \n \n(b) whether the Government has released a package under the incentive scheme for \ntextile workers; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether the Government is considering the grant of special package for the \nwelfare of textile workers in various needy States including Rajasthan; and  \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र      मंरी (श्री           \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TEXTILES \n(SHRI AJAY TAMTA) \n \n(a) to (c): The following welfare schemes  for Textile workers  are being \nimplemented by the Ministry of Textiles:- \n  \n (i) Direct Benefit to Artisans  for the Welfare of Artisans   is being \nimplemented for artisans in  the  Handicrafts  sector having the \nfollowing  sub components:- \n Rajiv Gandhi Shilpi Swasthaya Bima yojna for Health Insurance  \ncurrently under merger with Rashtriya Shilpi Swasthya Bima \nYojna. \n Aam A dmi Bima Yojna  providing  for life and accident \nInsurance \n Support to artisans in  indigent circumstances wherein award of \nRs 3500 is provided to Shilp Guru and National award /Nation \nmerit certificates recipients less than 60 years of age and earning \nless than Rs 50000 \n\n Credit Guarantee Scheme under which O/O DC Handicrafts pays \nguarantee fee to Credit Gaurantee Fund Trust for Micro and \nsmall enterprises for the coverage of  handicraft artisans. \n Mudra loan for Handicraft Artisans in line with Pradhan Mantri \nMudra yojna. \n Interest subvention scheme under which intere st subvention of \n6% provided to handicraft workers with a limit of Rs 100000 for \nthree years \n \n(ii) Handloom Weavers Comprehensive Welfare  Scheme  is being \nimplemented  for the Handloom Weavers having both health , life and \naccident insurance components  as available to the artisans in the \nhandicraft sector . Both the above programmes under merger with \nnational level programmes for health and life and accident insurance.  \nSeperately, the Ministry also implements the National Handloom \nDevelopment Programme, the Yarn Supply Scheme as well as the \nComprehensive Handloom Cluster Development programme under \nwhich  the Ministry provides  interalia interest subvention, margin \nmoney, credit guarantee, yarn at  subsidized rates etc to the  handloom \nweavers. \n \n(iii) Group Insurance Scheme for Powerloom workers: The scheme \nprovides insurance cover in the case of natural death accidental death \nas well as permanent and partial disability due to accident.  The scheme    \nis being implemented since  2003-04.  \n \n(iv) Under the special package provided for garments and \nmadeups, t he government additionally provides  3.67% share of \nemployer’s contribution  towards provident fund in addition to  the \n8.33%  being provided  under Pradhan Mantri Rozgar P rotsahan Yojna \nfor all new employees enrolling in EPFO for the first three years  .The \nGovernment has also made  employees’ contribution to EPF optional \nfor employees earning less than Rs 15000 per month  under the special \npackage for garments and madeups. \n \n(d) & (e): All the above mentioned schemes and incentives are being \nimplemented by Government on a pan India basis, including in Rajasthan. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |908", "t": "Vacant Posts of Coaches", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.908 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2017 \nVacant Posts of Coaches \n†908. SHRI BHAIRON PRASAD MISHRA:  \n Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased \nto state:  \n(a)  the details of vacant posts of coaches in various sports \nparticularly in which participants have won medals at National and \nInternational levels; and  \n(b)  the time by which these vac ant posts are likely to be filled \nparticularly in view of the targets for winning medals at \ninternational level? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR \nYOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI VIJAY GOEL) \n(a) & (b) : There are 1524 sanc tioned posts of Coaches in all \ncategories in Sports Authority of India i.e. Chief Coach, Senior \nCoach, Coach and Assistant Coach. Out of the a bove, at present, \n484 posts of Coaches are vacant in Sports Authority of India. There \nare no discipline-wise sanctioned posts of Coaches in SAI. \n \nThe recruitment process is in progress and the time period to \nfill up the vacant posts cannot be fixed. \n*** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |909", "t": "Funds under PMKSY", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES,\nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 909\nANSWERED ON 20.07.2017\nFUNDS UNDER PMKSY\n909. SHRIMATI ANJU BALA\nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA\nREJUVENATION be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has launched Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchai Yojana (PMKSY) and\nallocated funds for Uttar Pradesh thereunder to boost up irrigati on facilities in rural areas of the\nState; and\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the budgetary allocations made during the last year for each district\nof the State with special reference to Hardoi and Sitapur?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND\nGANGA REJUVENATION\n(DR. SANJEEV KUMAR BALYAN)\n(a) & (b) Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY) was launched during the year 2015 -16\nin the country including Uttar Pradesh, to enhance physical access of water on farm and expand\ncultivable area under assured irrigation, improve on farm water use efficiency and introduce\nsustainable water conservation practices, etc.\nUnder PMKSY-Accelerated Irrigation Benefits Programme (AIBP), 4 irrigation projects of\nUttar Pradesh have been identified in consultation with state, for completion in phases up to\nDecember, 2019. For completion of these projects in a mission mode, funding mechanism through\nNABARD has been approved by the Go vernment for both Central as well as State share. Details of\nCentral Assistance (CA) released to these projects during 2016-17 are as under:\nName of the Project CA released (In Cr.)\nBansagar Irrigation Project 64.64\nArjun Sahayak Irrigation Project 8.99\nSaryu Nahar Irrigation Project 62.00\nFurther, project proposal for CA release w.r.t. Madhya Ganga Irrigation Project was not\nreceived in the Ministry.\nDetails of CA released to Uttar Pradesh during 2016 -17 under other components PMKSY are\nas under:\nComponents CA released (In Cr.)#\nPer Drop More Crop 41.20\nWatershed Development 58.38\n# Inter se distribution of funds amongst the projects/districts in a particular State is decided\nby the respective States.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |91", "t": "Development of Heritage Sites as Tourist Spots", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 91ST\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 21ST JULY, 2017\nASHA TRAINING PROJECT\n*91. SHRI PARESH RAVAL:\nSHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether the Government has signed Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) with\nNational Institute of Open Schooling (NIOS) and provided them with funds to run\nthe Accredited Social Health Activists (ASHA) Training Project;\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the salient features of the MoU;\n(c) whether the Government has given time limit to NIOS for completing the ASHA\nTraining Project and if so, the details thereof; and\n(d) the details of funds allocated and utilised by NIOS during the last three years and\nthe current year?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (d) : A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 91* FOR 21ST JULY, 2017\n(a) & (b): A Tripartite Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) was signed between Ministry of\nHealth & Family Welfare, (MoHFW) National Institute of Open Schooling (NIOS) and National\nHealth Systems Resource Centre (NHSRC) in 2014 for implementation of Certification of ASHA\nand Accreditation of associated Agencies in ASHA training under the National Health Mission.\nFunds have been provided to NIOS for undertaking process of accreditation of trainers & training\nsites and certification of ASHAs . However, NIOS is not directly involved in the training of\nASHAs.\nThe salient features of the MoU are as below:-\ni. Accreditation/certification by NIOS of four key program components namely - Training\ncurriculum, Training Sites, Trainers, ASHAs and ASHA Facilitators.\nii. MoHFW is to provide o verall policy support to the process and provide funding support to\nNIOS and NHSRC.\niii. NHSRC is to facilitate co nstitution of ‘Technical Advisory Committee’ for\n‘Standardization of Training Curriculum’ which would be adapted by NIOS into 200 hours\nof vocational studies\niv. NIOS to develop Certification guidelines for Training Sites and Trainers , supplementary\nguides in Hindi, English and other regional languages and disseminate to the states and\ndistricts by NIOS\nv. Undertaking orientation workshop for states and planning for ASHA certification in NHM\nProgramme Implementation plans by NHSRC.\nvi. Registration of ASHAs and ASH A facilitators in the vocational program, conducting final\nexamination for ASHA and ASHA facilitators and issuing certificates by NIOS.\nvii. A Project Steering Committee as an advisory body for implementing the roll out of ASHA\nCertification.\n(c) The tripartite MOU has a time -frame of three years . However, the implementation of the\nprogramme is dependent upon the readiness of NIOS and states to implement the activities.\n(d) The details of funds allocated and utilized by NIOS during the last three year s and current year\nare as below:\nFinancial Year Funds released to NIOS Funds Utilised by NIOS\n2014-15 Rs 7,64,72,049/- -\n2015-16 - Rs 28,74,455/-\n2016-17 - Rs 56,10,983/-\n2017-18 (01.04.17 –\n30.06.17) - Rs 15,22,745/-"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |910", "t": "Rain Water Harvesting", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES,\nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †910\nANSWERED ON 20.07.2017\nRAIN WATER HARVESTING\n†910. SHRI LAXMI NARAYAN YADAV\nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA\nREJUVENATION be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has undertaken any works towards rain water harvesting in cities by\ndeveloping/evolving the rain water harvesting system techni que in cities, if so, the details thereof\nand if not, the reasons therefor;\n(b) the steps taken by the Government for making the rain water harvesting mandatory for all\nagencies in cities; and\n(c) the outcome/success achieved as a result of the same?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND\nGANGA REJUVENATION\n(DR. SANJEEV KUMAR BALYAN)\n(a) to (c) Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) has prepared a Manual on Art ificial Recharge to\nGroundwater which presents various techniques on artificial recharge and rainwater harvesting for\nrural as well as u rban areas. Further, CGWB has also prepared a conceptual document entitled\n“Master Plan for Artificial Recharge to Ground Water, 2013 ”, which envisages construction of\ndifferent types of artificial recharge structures in the Country including those in urban areas.\nCentral Ground Water Authority ( CGWA) has issued advisories to States and UTs to take\nmeasures to promote/adopt artificial recharge to ground water / rain water harvesting. So far, 30\nStates/UTs have made rain water harvesting mandatory by enacting laws or by formulating rules &\nregulations or by including provisions in building bye-laws or through suitable Government Orders.\nMinistry of Urban Development has released Model Building Bye Laws (MBBL) in March ,\n2016, which is an advisory document for the State/UT Governments .  MBBL, 2016 has pr ovisions\nof r ain water harvesting in different building categories such as in residential, public and semi -\npublic building, commercial and industrial.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |912", "t": "Per Capita Availability of Drinking Water", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 912 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2017 \n \nPer Capita Availability of Drinking Water \n \n912. DR. SHASHI THAROOR: \n \nWill the Minister of DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether Kerala is one of the States with lowes t per capita access to safe drinking water \nin the country;  \n(b)  if so, whether the Government is monitoring th e situation of deteriorating supply of \ndrinking water in the event of drought situation in  the State and if so, thedetails thereof \nand if not, the reasons therefor; and \n (c)  whether his Ministry proposes to provide fina ncial assistance to the State under the \ncalamity component of the National Rural Drinking W ater Programme and if so, the \ndetails thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n(SHRI RAMESH CHANDAPPA JIGAJINAGI) \n(a) As per the data reported by the States on Integrate d Management Information System \n(IMIS) of this Ministry with respect to status of coverage in terms of availability of at least 40 \nlitres per capita per day safe drinking water, Kera la has the lowest coverage. % of population \nresiding in Fully Covered Habitation (i.e. getting more than 40 litre per capita par day safe \ndrinking water) is 22.06 %.  \n(b) & (c) This Ministry is monitoring the situation  and accordingly advises the state \ngovernment to prioritize for completing various ong oing schemes under National Rural \nDrinking Water Programme (NRDWP) for improving the coverage and supplementing \nthrough the funding from the state budget. Further by taking cognizance of the predictions of \nIndian Meteorological Department (IMD), this Ministry use to advise concerned states to take \nimmediate steps to tackle the scarcity of drinking water as per the Standard Operating \nProcedure (SOP) meant for such natural calamities. The States may also take use of flexi fund \nto the extent of 25 % of every releases under NRDWP made to the States in such situations. \nMoreover, under NRDWP, there is also a provision fo r assisting the States in the event of \nNatural Calamity which inter-alia includes drought. 2% of the NRDWP allocation is set aside \nfor any natural calamity including drought. On the declaration of drought, Inter Ministerial \nCentral Team (IMCT) visits the affected States and gives its recommendations regarding the \nquantum of assistance to be given. Funds are therea fter released to State with the approval of \na High level Committee (HLC) constituted in the Ministry of Home Affairs. However, during \nthe current financial year, as on 17.07.2017, no re commendation of HLC has been received, \nand accordingly, no funds have been released to the  State Government of Kerala under this \ncomponent.  \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|12|UNSTARRED |913", "t": "Trauma Care on NHs", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 913 \nANSWERED ON 20th July, 2017 \n \nTRAUMA CARE ON NHs \n \n913.   SHRI E.T. MOHAMMED BASHEER: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has launched any pilot project for trauma care on National \nHighways across the country; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof, State-wise; \n \n(c)  the status of projects now in existence in co-operating with the State Governments; and \n \n(d) the steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n (SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a) to (d ) Yes, Madam. Ministry of Health & Family Welfare has launched a scheme under \nwhich financial assistance are provided to the State Government for Trauma Care facilities in \nthe Government Hospitals located on Na tional Highways.  During 11 th Five Year Plan, 116 \nTrauma Care Facilities were funded to establish Trauma Care Facilities at various level with \n100% central funding along the Golden Quadrilateral Highways Corridor as well as North -\nSouth and East - West Cor ridor.  During 12 th  Five Year Plan, 81 hospitals have been \nidentified on National Highways for providing the facilities of Trauma Care. The state wise \ndetails are annexed. \n \n Since April 2015, the financial assistance is shared between Central and State \nGovernment in the ratio of 60:40 onwards; the ratio of sharing for North Eastern States and \nhilly States of Himachal Pradesh, Uttrakhand and Jammu & Kashmir is 90:10; for UTs th ere \nis 100% central funding.   \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure \nSTATE WISE AND CORRIDOR WISE LIST OF 116 TRAUMA CENTERS IDENTIFIED DURING 11 TH PLAN \nSl.No. \n \n \nCorridor \nwise \n \nName of Trauma Centre \nLevel \nA – HOSPITALS UNDER STATE GOVERNMENT  \n  \n  ANDHRA PRADESH   \n1 \nN-S \nGovt. General Hospital &Medical College, Kurnool II \n2 Govt. General Hospital &Medical College, Anantapur II \n3 Community Hospital, Penukonda III \n4 \nG-Q \nTaluka Hospital, Tekkali,  III \n5 District Hospital, Srikakulam II  \n6 King George Hospital & Andhra Medical College, Visakhapatnam II \n7 Taluk Hospital, Tuni, East Godavari III \n8 District Hospital, Rajahmundry, East Godavari II \n9 District Hospital, Eluru, West Godavari III \n10 Medical College, Guntur II \n11 District Hospital, Ongole,  III \n12 Dist. Hospital, Nellore II \n13 Taluk Hospital, Nayadupet III \n    ASSAM   \n14 \nE-W \nMedical College & Hospital, Silchar. II \n15 Civil Hospital, Haflong. III \n16 Civil Hospital, Diphu. III \n17 District Hospital, Naogaon. II \n18 Medical College